    "q-97745": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97745",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number of correct answers in a multiple choice test of $10$ questions, where each answer is either \"true\" or \"false,\" and each answer is guessed randomly.\n\nII. The number obtained when spinning a fair roulette wheel.\n\nIII. The initial letter of a word picked at random from an English dictionary.",
      "worked_solution": "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each number of correct answers is not equally likely. For example, it is more likely to score $50%$ than $100%.$\n\n- Random variable II can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. For example, each outcome of a roulette wheel is represented by a number between $0$ and $37,$ and since the roulette is fair, each number is equally likely.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because some letters are more common than others.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
