    "q-148408": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148408",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "When rolling a fair six-sided die, what is the probability of getting a prime number?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the number on which the die lands. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{1,2,…,6\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\in \\{2,3,5\\}).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{1,2,…,6\\},$ and the set $S=\\{1,2,…,6\\}$ has $|S|=6$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}, & x=1,2,…,6 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X\\in \\{2,3,5\\}$ are $2,3,5.$ There are $3$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\in \\{2,3,5\\})=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
