    "q-97647": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97647",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "A child is playing with a tablet. The apps on the tablet are labeled with numbers $4$ through $21.$ If the child chooses an app at random, what is the probability that the app number they pick is more than $16?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the number of the app picked. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{4,5,…,21\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X>16).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{4,5,…,21\\},$ and the set $S=\\{4,5,…,21\\}$ has $|S|=21-4+1=18$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18}, & x=4,5,…,21 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X>16$ are $17,18,19,20,21.$ There are $5$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>16)=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}=\\frac{5}{18}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
